// Data of the theorem graph (theorems.html): theorems, lemmas, corollaries, axioms and conjectures of mathematical
// physics, MSC 2020 70, 74, 76, 78, 80, 81, 82, 83, 85 and 86. In English. Built on 3 November 2026. Moved here by rule 1
// of ../../CLAUDE.md, with its id: `no-ghost-theorem` (81T30) from ../../representation-theory/data/theorem-graph.js.
//
// Model as in ../../analysis/data/theorem-graph.js (see its header comment): `premises` and `proof` are the standard
// textbook proof, named in `proofName`; further proofs in `alternativeProofs`; `foundation: true` marks a proof that
// rests on theory outside these graphs (named in `proof`), also together with premises. `area` is one of the groupings
// of data/concepts.js (one per main MSC field), `msc5` the five-character class. A few results whose home folder exists
// but does not have them yet (Stone's theorem 47D03, the spectral theorem for unbounded operators 47B25, the
// Kato–Rellich theorem 47A55) stand here with the area "basic" and should move to analysis.
// The two laws of thermodynamics are axioms (kind "axiom"), as the axioms of set theory: the theorems of classical
// thermodynamics are derived from them. Elsewhere the physical laws (Newton's, Maxwell's, Einstein's and Schrödinger's
// equations) are definitions of the models (data/concepts.js), and the theorems are statements about these models.
// The computations of physics (differentiating along a solution, inserting an ansatz into an equation) count as use of
// definitions, as in ../../geometry/ (noether-symplectic).
// References to theorems of other folders: "st:", "co:", "rt:", "top:", "ana:", "geo:", and the folders built at the same
// time: calculus of variations "cv:" (Hamilton's principle, Noether's theorem, Hamilton's canonical equations), dynamical
// systems "ds:" (Liouville–Arnold, KAM), differential equations "de:" (Leray–Hopf, Caffarelli–Kohn–Nirenberg, Navier–Stokes
// conjecture), probability "pr:" (percolation, Feynman–Kac, Jensen).
//
// Sources: see data/concepts.js; further Moser, Stable and Random Motions in Dynamical Systems (1973); Saari, Collisions,
// Rings, and Other Newtonian N-Body Problems (2005); Bertozzi and Majda, Vorticity and Incompressible Flow (2002); Glimm
// and Jaffe, Quantum Physics (2nd ed. 1987); Duminil-Copin, Lectures on the Ising and Potts models (2017); Grimmett,
// Percolation (2nd ed. 1999); Christodoulou and Klainerman, The Global Nonlinear Stability of the Minkowski Space (1993).
// Not checked by a specialist. Checked by ../../tests/check-theorems.js.
// `fromDefinitions: true` marks a theorem whose usual proof uses only definitions and axioms of its area and no
// theorem (it has no premises); a theorem without premises that rests on theory outside these graphs carries
// `foundation: true`, and its `proof` names that theory.

